    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_01fdbe6dd4589cd3968481b5aecc86d52e80fcafe8a8efa703a620f9f080f7e8",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Multiplicativity and the prime-adic case table turn G_k(n) into a local support-and-amplitude calculus: for coprime odd moduli the factors separate, and for p^beta the valuation alpha=v_p(k) determines whether the factor vanishes or takes a totient, signed p-power, or quadratic-character value. The gain is that a composite-modulus value can be assembled from sparse local rules; the loss is that literal constants and signs inherit the display's extraction risk.",
